00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:26,100
 Free articulate speaker understands five qualities about speech that allow them to be playful,

26
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:29,440
 precise and persuasive with their words.

27
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:32,920
 The first is expanding their surface lexicon.

28
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:36,260
 Your lexicon refers to your personal vocabulary.

29
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:40,400
 The collection of words you recognize and employ in your speaking.

30
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:45,000
 Each of us has a surface lexicon and a deep lexicon.

31
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,640
 I'll use my proprietary whiteboard technology to illustrate this.

32
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:54,440
 Your surface lexicon, illustrated here, are the words you unconsciously default to and

33
00:01:54,440 --> 00:02:00,800
 employ in your daily communication, approximately 1500 to 3000 unique words.

34
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:06,000
 Your deep lexicon, on the other hand, is comprised of words that you recognize but rarely

35
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:07,680
 employ in your speech.

36
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:13,080
 The average native English speaking adult has a deep lexicon of approximately 20,000 to

37
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:15,640
 35,000 unique words.

38
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:19,840
 Can we speak when we answer a question or compose a sentence?

39
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:24,440
 Our brain first consults our surface lexicon for familiar words.

40
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:28,120
 For example, if I asked you, what did you think of the steak?

41
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:30,920
 The default reply might be, the steak was good.

42
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:32,680
 It was awesome.

43
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:37,720
 Have you heard of the words flavorful, tender or perfectly seasoned?

44
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:38,720
 Of course.

45
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:42,480
 But those words are not located in your surface lexicon.

46
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:48,160
 And thus, not the language that your brain defaults to, as descriptors for a steak.

47
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:53,440
 What we realize is that the default way our brain wants us to say something is more than

48
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,640
 likely not the best way to say it.

49
00:02:55,640 --> 00:03:00,960
 Because the words registered in our surface lexicon are simply not the right words.

50
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:05,600
 And that's what articulacy is, being able to express your ideas in the right words

51
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,480
 at the right time.

52
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:12,440
 This invites the question, how do we access this deep lexicon that we all have?

53
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:22,680
 How do we give the words we already know more buoyancy so that they register in our

54
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:27,000
 surface lexicon and begin to manifest in conversation?

55
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,640
 Let me provide you with two solutions that accomplish this.

56
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:31,820
 The first is repetition.

57
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:35,520
 The more you use a word, the higher it ranks in your lexicon.

58
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:41,000
 A study from Yale found that it takes using a word 38 unique times for our brain to instinctively

59
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,600
 default to that word in our speech.

60
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:49,320
 In other words, 38 times in order for that word to register in our surface lexicon.

61
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:53,640
 What you must realize about our ticketless speakers is that they aren't constantly accessing

62
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:55,000
 their deep lexicon.

63
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,480
 Rather, they've expanded their surface lexicon to include words that are more profound

64
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,000
 and expressive.

65
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 You'll observe this if you watch any speaker for hours on end.

66
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,400
 Any words you might consider to be powerful or profound will surface frequently in their

67
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:18,560
 speaking, but often use words that carry imagery or embody tangible ideas.

68
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:23,000
 For example, instead of saying, "I have to pay attention to a lot of information," they

69
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,840
 might say, "I have to pay attention to a mosaic of information."

70
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:34,120
 Or instead of saying, "I'm at a lack of ideas," they'll say, "I've reached idea poverty."

71
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:39,720
 Besides repetition, using a word multiple times in order for it to possibly enter or rank

72
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:45,320
 higher in your surface lexicon, the other action you can take is to give yourself more

73
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:49,240
 time to index your deep lexicon.

74
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:51,120
 What do I mean by this?

75
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:52,720
 Think of it like breathing.

76
00:04:52,720 --> 00:05:00,360
 You can use shallow breaths and still provide your lungs and brain with oxygen.

77
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:11,040
 Or you can take your time, inhale deeply, and feel more satisfied and replenished.

78
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:13,560
 Breathe deeper with your words.

79
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:19,280
 Take more time before you speak to index your vocabulary for better words.

80
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:22,280
 Ask yourself this question.

81
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:24,800
 Is this the right word?

82
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:26,200
 Is this the right word?

83
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:31,500
 If I took more time, a few more seconds, could I perhaps excavate a better word from

84
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,840
 my deep lexicon?

85
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:41,800
 You'll be amazed how much progress you make in your speaking just by asking yourself

86
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:43,880
 this introspective question.

87
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:45,400
 Is this the right word?

88
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:47,680
 Is there a better word for this idea?

89
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:52,280
 Don't assume the word your brain defaults to is the best word.

90
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:56,240
 Number two, learn how to command the pause.

91
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:01,160
 One of the epiphany moments I experienced in working to elevate my own speech was learning

92
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:09,280
 that I could make ordinary words sound extraordinary simply by using a well placed pause.

93
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:16,240
 The recommendation to use pauses is often considered trivial because it's so simple.

94
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:23,400
 Yet being articulate requires that you understand this relationship between silence and the

95
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:26,480
 impact that it has in your speech.

96
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:31,400
 As a society, we've almost become allergic to pausing in our speaking.

97
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:38,280
 We think that pausing is considered a sign of mental slowness or unintelligence.

98
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:41,120
 When most of the time, it conveys the opposite.

99
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:46,840
 Confidence shows that you value the weight of your words and wish to be calculated and

100
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:48,880
 intentional with your language.

101
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:53,640
 It shows confidence in yourself and care for your listener.

102
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:59,760
 Watch this short clip from Steve Jobs and take notice of how pausing before speaking

103
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:05,960
 creates suspense in the listener's mind but more importantly allows Steve time to construct

104
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:08,880
 a confident and articulate answer.

105
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:17,600
 That's important to you in the development of a product.

106
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:28,320
 You know, one of the things that really hurt Apple was after I left John Scully got a

107
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:36,160
 very serious disease and that disease, I've seen other people get it too, it's the disease

108
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:41,640
 of thinking that a really great idea is 90% of the work.

109
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:48,200
 As Steve demonstrates, silence before you speak allows you to formulate your thoughts more

110
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:49,480
 fully.

111
00:07:49,480 --> 00:07:56,040
 Most of us in conversation or any form of communication have the proclivity to introduce words before

112
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:57,800
 we know how we're going to use them.

113
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:00,760
 Sometimes I'll start a sentence and I don't even know where it's going.

114
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:08,480
 We blurt out a hasty first pass at conveying an idea and then progressively revise our word

115
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:14,720
 choice by rephrasing our sentence or perhaps appending on more words which furthers the

116
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:21,720
 lack of clarity of the idea or attempting to convey pausing before speaking or between

117
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:28,720
 points allows you to achieve a better intimacy with your words.

118
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:33,260
 You have more time to think through your ideas and your listener is able to thoroughly

119
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:36,960
 absorb your words because they are more deliberate.

120
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:39,280
 Your words are more precise.

121
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:42,400
 Number three, pruning your filler words.

122
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:48,320
 I would imagine most of us already know we shouldn't over rely on words such as "ah"

123
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:53,920
 and "like" or expressions like "you know" I feel like basically, essentially.

124
00:08:53,920 --> 00:08:58,240
 The full list is available down below this video if you want to diagnose your disfluencies

125
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,360
 as filler words are often known as.

126
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:04,560
 It's worth noting that filler words are in fact a natural part of language.

127
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:11,520
 As ironic as it may be, filler words help our speech flow and sound less formal and blunt.

128
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:15,520
 The thorny problem though is when we overwork them in our speech.

129
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:19,840
 Filler words appear because we often speak faster than we can think.

130
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:22,960
 Our mouth gets ahead of our mind and we need to catch up.

131
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:25,080
 And hence we rely on the filler word.

132
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:29,520
 Also because we're uncomfortable with silence and we'll do anything to maintain command

133
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:33,880
 of the air even if it means treating our listener to an empty sound.

134
00:09:33,880 --> 00:09:38,560
 I've been able to cure myself of most filler words by adopting two practices that I want

135
00:09:38,560 --> 00:09:39,560
 to share with you.

136
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:45,200
 The first is processing the endings of your words.

137
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:50,800
 When I focused on hearing the endings of the words that are coming out of my mouth,

138
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:54,800
 it automatically reduces the speed of my speech.

139
00:09:54,800 --> 00:10:01,440
 But two, channels my focus onto the words themselves, allowing me to audit my word selection

140
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:02,440
 more carefully.

141
00:10:02,440 --> 00:10:08,640
 Secondly, practice keeping your mouth closed until you are ready to speak your first word.

142
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:15,000
 For many of us, the default behavior is to introduce our voice with a filler.

143
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:21,520
 For example, Joseph, what did you think of this project?

144
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:22,880
 Is that familiar?

145
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:29,800
 If so, keep your mouth closed until you have defined in your mind your opening words.

146
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:32,240
 This has helped me immensely.

147
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:37,160
 Number four, take inventory of your language inputs.

148
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:39,600
 What do I mean by this?

149
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:45,200
 I mean, recognize that the way you speak right now is the result of the collective influence

150
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:48,240
 of the language that you've been exposed to.

151
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:54,680
 Just as we are often the product of our environments, our speech reflects the quality of language

152
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:59,720
 that we've interacted with, and that we've allowed into our information processor, our

153
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:01,720
 language processor.

154
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:07,880
 If you read and listen to cheap and shoddy sentences filled with slang, vulgarity, and vague

155
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:13,360
 vocabulary, you unconsciously adopt those language patterns, and the higher and higher

156
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:19,360
 those expressions and words register in your surface lexicon until they begin to automatically

157
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:21,520
 manifest in our speech.

158
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:27,080
 This is why it's advantageous to read and expose yourself to sources of rich language.

159
00:11:27,080 --> 00:11:32,240
 There are a few great articulators who aren't ridiculously well read for this very reason.

160
00:11:32,240 --> 00:11:38,400
 Reading great works exposes you to great words, and expands your intellectual horizon,

161
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:41,760
 which enriches your thinking and reflects in your speech.

162
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:44,800
 Of course, I'm not just talking about reading books.

163
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:50,840
 My recommendation is to take inventory of all the sources of language you expose yourself

164
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:52,720
 to on a daily basis.

165
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:57,880
 The people you surround yourself with, the music you listen to, the content you consume.

166
00:11:57,880 --> 00:11:59,640
 You don't have to make any drastic changes.

167
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:03,920
 I'm not recommending you overhaul your life just because you watched a YouTube video.

168
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:10,480
 But if you do notice that most sources where you watch or listen to words don't feature

169
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:15,120
 articulate or expressive language, I might suggest engaging more with sources of articulate

170
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:23,200
 speech that give you better access to the tapestry of language that you seek to embody in

171
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:24,760
 your speech.

172
00:12:24,760 --> 00:12:28,600
 Number five, tune your vocal instrument.

173
00:12:28,600 --> 00:12:34,880
 As much as we like to define articulacy as choosing the right words, we often grossly

174
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:40,880
 undervalue the significance that our voice holds in sounding articulate.

175
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:45,400
 What you'll notice in articulate speakers is that there is a music and a melodic quality

176
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:48,080
 to articulating their words.

177
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:56,200
 Words aren't just words, they're receptacles that are filled with emotional substance,

178
00:12:56,200 --> 00:13:03,520
 and that substance is provided by our pitch or volume, the speed of our speaking and variation.

179
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:08,640
 The same word spoken in a monotone can sound staggeringly different than if they were

180
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:12,120
 delivered with finesse and maturity.

181
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:16,520
 Listen to this clip from Sir David Attenborough, a man considered to have one of the most beloved

182
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:18,120
 voices of all time.

183
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:24,160
 I want you to pay attention to the variation in his voice, his pausing, and above all the

184
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:27,440
 complete enunciation of each word.

185
00:13:27,440 --> 00:13:37,080
 As you spend the next two weeks debating, negotiating, persuading, and compromising as you surely

186
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:44,480
 must, it's easy to forget that ultimately the emergency time that comes down to a single

187
00:13:44,480 --> 00:13:45,520
 number.

