00:13:45,520 --> 00:13:51,400
 Of course, while not everyone of us can acquire a rich voice or adopt an elegant British accent,

189
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:55,460
 we can learn to be more melodic with our voices.

190
00:13:55,460 --> 00:13:58,560
 Learn to think of your sentences almost like music.

191
00:13:58,560 --> 00:14:04,880
 Each word is a note that harmonizes with the others to produce a beautiful melody that

192
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:08,040
 can enhance the enjoyment of listening to you.

193
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:13,680
 Perhaps the quickest way to help develop your vocal instrument is to read poetry out loud.

194
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:21,320
 Poetry is incredibly effective at subtly teaching how vital variation in your tone is,

195
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:25,600
 because you have to read poems a certain way, often with playful emphasis on specific

196
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:29,400
 words in order to achieve the rhyme set up in the poem.

197
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:33,240
 I'll leave a list of the best poems below this video that will help with this.

198
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:38,720
 Please practice these five techniques discussed in this video, and you will become more athletic

199
00:14:38,720 --> 00:14:41,320
 and artistic with your language.

200
00:14:41,320 --> 00:14:46,000
 I read these six books on speaking articulately, and I want to share three astonishing secrets

201
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,920
 and three resources with you that have allowed me to finally solve my problem of being

202
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:53,520
 unable to articulate my thoughts.

203
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:56,760
 The first secret is to share more connotation.

204
00:14:56,760 --> 00:15:03,760
 Connotation refers to the multitude of experiences, emotions, associations, memories, meaning,

205
00:15:03,760 --> 00:15:07,480
 and images that are packed behind our words.

206
00:15:07,480 --> 00:15:08,880
 Let's visualize this.

207
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:14,640
 If someone speaks about war, that idea of war is the representation of many feelings passing

208
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:16,000
 through their mind.

209
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:20,880
 They may have seen more, read about war, watched war films, and know that people associate

210
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:23,280
 war with tragedy and death.

211
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:28,540
 The combination of all these various elements creates the language of the mind, what

212
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:32,240
 psycho-lingual Stephen Pinker called "mental ease."

213
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:39,520
 Speaking is our attempt of translating mental ease into the low bandwidth mechanism that

214
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:41,360
 is the spoken word.

215
00:15:41,360 --> 00:15:47,600
 Our listener receives those words and unfolds them into the experiences, associations,

216
00:15:47,600 --> 00:15:51,680
 emotions, memories, and images that that word has for them.

217
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:58,280
 Think of this process like a musical composer experiencing emotion, and channeling that emotion

218
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:00,240
 into writing sheet music.

219
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:05,240
 An orchestra conductor can read the sheet music and guide the orchestra and transform those

220
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:11,000
 static notes on page into a lively, powerful performance of Beethoven's Symphony No. 5.

221
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:18,440
 The emotions that we experience listening to this live music cannot possibly be represented

222
00:16:18,440 --> 00:16:23,720
 by simple black and white notation on paper.

223
00:16:23,720 --> 00:16:29,800
 Frustration with articulation occurs when we expect our connotation to be fully represented

224
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:35,120
 by our words and transplanted somehow in our listener's mind, which is frankly an

225
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:43,560
 impossible ask, translating our 40 thoughts into what really are 1D words requires compromise,

226
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:50,680
 which often results in that familiar sensation of not being able to express ourselves satisfactorily.

227
00:16:50,680 --> 00:16:56,680
 These books make it very clear that language simply does not give us the full capacity

228
00:16:56,680 --> 00:17:05,200
 to thoroughly express what we're thinking, feeling, fearing, wanting in a given moment.

229
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:06,840
 But we can't get close.

230
00:17:06,840 --> 00:17:15,000
 This invites the question, how can we communicate more connotation so that one, our words better

231
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:21,080
 capture what it is that's on our mind, and two, our listener can unfold our words into

232
00:17:21,080 --> 00:17:24,640
 something that closely resembles what we experience.

233
00:17:24,640 --> 00:17:27,040
