931
01:05:07,520 --> 01:05:14,120
 new ground, I'm retracing previous verbal territory with replications and variations

932
01:05:14,120 --> 01:05:15,120
 of the same word.

933
01:05:15,120 --> 01:05:19,480
 You might think I'm being scrupulous here, but pruning redundancy spirals from your

934
01:05:19,480 --> 01:05:24,160
 speaking is one of the chief contributors to creating clean speech.

935
01:05:24,160 --> 01:05:27,080
 There's a crisp quality to sentences without it.

936
01:05:27,080 --> 01:05:35,520
 In fact, what makes the words of great articulators so penetrating is the amount of effort they've

937
01:05:35,520 --> 01:05:41,440
 put into unlearning poor speech habits like redundancy spirals and like the third type

938
01:05:41,440 --> 01:05:44,760
 of fatty language intensifiers.

939
01:05:44,760 --> 01:05:50,560
 Intensifiers are empty words that we use to cushion our adjectives in sentences.

940
01:05:50,560 --> 01:05:56,600
 Kind of, sort of, actually, really, basically, virtually, literally, super, pretty much.

941
01:05:56,600 --> 01:05:58,440
 Our trip was really fantastic.

942
01:05:58,440 --> 01:06:00,320
 I literally just saw them at the store.

943
01:06:00,320 --> 01:06:03,040
 I kind of want to go to that Mediterranean restaurant.

944
01:06:03,040 --> 01:06:08,200
 The trouble with intensifiers is that they pad our sentences with uncertainty and cheap

945
01:06:08,200 --> 01:06:09,880
 in the meaning of our words.

946
01:06:09,880 --> 01:06:12,240
 It's not enough to say an event was fantastic.

947
01:06:12,240 --> 01:06:14,920
 It has to be pretty fantastic to be fantastic.

948
01:06:14,920 --> 01:06:18,760
 Not of these fatty language habits are word crimes against humanity.

949
01:06:18,760 --> 01:06:23,680
 99% of us communicate using bloated language, including myself.

950
01:06:23,680 --> 01:06:29,200
 But if you want to achieve greater exactness with your speaking, I suggest three actions.

951
01:06:29,200 --> 01:06:34,440
 Number one is to keep your mouth closed until you've defined your starting words.

952
01:06:34,440 --> 01:06:40,240
 The most vulnerable moment, particularly for filler words, is at the start of our sentences.

953
01:06:40,240 --> 01:06:47,320
 Keeping your mouth closed prevents your brain from defaulting to that initial um, that

954
01:06:47,320 --> 01:06:49,880
 your mind so desperately wants you to lead with.

955
01:06:49,880 --> 01:06:55,840
 As it relates to redundancy spirals and intensifiers, the best solution I found is simply awareness.

956
01:06:55,840 --> 01:07:03,240
 The challenge is how do we keep our mind focused on stripping our sentences of bloated language

957
01:07:03,240 --> 01:07:07,960
 without taking away cognitive horsepower from communicating our thoughts?

958
01:07:07,960 --> 01:07:15,360
 I noticed this when I was modifying my own speaking behaviors and I invented two solutions.

959
01:07:15,360 --> 01:07:19,240
 Number one is to process the endings of your words.

960
01:07:19,240 --> 01:07:24,880
 When you hear the endings of your words, you can't help but focus on what it is you're

961
01:07:24,880 --> 01:07:26,120
 saying.

962
01:07:26,120 --> 01:07:29,080
 This results in more calculated speech.

963
01:07:29,080 --> 01:07:31,960
 The second solution, create a finger tab.

964
01:07:31,960 --> 01:07:37,920
 This practice consists of pinching your index finger and thumb together as a way of keeping

965
01:07:37,920 --> 01:07:44,600
 your mind focused on reducing redundancy spirals and intensifiers in your speaking.

966
01:07:44,600 --> 01:07:50,200
 When you create that pinch and you hold it as you speak, that mild discomfort creates

967
01:07:50,200 --> 01:07:55,120
 an open tab in your mind that causes you to audit your speech more carefully.

968
01:07:55,120 --> 01:07:59,320
 If I'm just stipulating a lot with my hands, what I will often do as an alternative to

969
01:07:59,320 --> 01:08:02,480
 the finger tab is scrunch my toes and my shoes.

970
01:08:02,480 --> 01:08:06,680
 You only have to do this until you retrain your mind to speak concisely.

971
01:08:06,680 --> 01:08:11,520
 If you want to be articulate like world-class podcasters, speakers and leaders, I'm going

972
01:08:11,520 --> 01:08:16,800
 to share the seven-stage journey that has taught me to speak with greater exactness and

973
01:08:16,800 --> 01:08:17,800
 clarity.

974
01:08:17,800 --> 01:08:21,680
 It includes understanding three problems invading your speech.

975
01:08:21,680 --> 01:08:27,520
 The a-ha moment that immediately will improve your choice of words and three thinking modifications

976
01:08:27,520 --> 01:08:32,240
 that brought a verbal freshness and intelligence to my sentences.

977
01:08:32,240 --> 01:08:36,600
 The first problem was my addiction to overusing dead phrases.

978
01:08:36,600 --> 01:08:43,320
 Dead phrases are phrases and expressions that we use in such a recreational way that they

979
01:08:43,320 --> 01:08:45,880
 have been stripped of all power in life.

980
01:08:45,880 --> 01:08:51,880
 Popular dead phrases include pursue that avenue, the Achilles heel, secret sauce to say the

981
01:08:51,880 --> 01:08:54,760
 least, ins and outs, spill the beans.

982
01:08:54,760 --> 01:09:00,000
 We use dead phrases because they save us the time and trouble of hunting for more precise

983
01:09:00,000 --> 01:09:01,000
 words.

984
01:09:01,000 --> 01:09:06,000
 This introduces the first of seven articulacy rules, rule number one, articulacy increases

985
01:09:06,000 --> 01:09:09,080
 when you practice conscious selection with your words.

986
01:09:09,080 --> 01:09:14,920
 If you hastily choose ready-made phrases and gum them together in your sentences like

987
01:09:14,920 --> 01:09:20,720
 the sections of a prefabricated Ikea table, they didn't endow your sentence.

988
01:09:20,720 --> 01:09:30,040
 Ultimately, you want to learn how to engineer sentences that are fresh, homemade and excavate

989
01:09:30,040 --> 01:09:35,920
 that richness and texture that often gets buried beneath dead phrases.

990
01:09:35,920 --> 01:09:40,440
 The second problem holding me back was my small surface lexicon.

991
01:09:40,440 --> 01:09:45,080
 The term lexicon refers to your personal vocabulary.

992
01:09:45,080 --> 01:09:52,100
 Your surface lexicon are those 500 to 1500 words that your brain unconsciously and effortlessly

993
01:09:52,100 --> 01:09:55,680
 defaults to when you compose sentences.

994
01:09:55,680 --> 01:09:59,080
 The concert was amazing, it was a really good book.

995
01:09:59,080 --> 01:10:06,800
 These words, you've heard, seen, said so often, they ooze out of your mouth.

996
01:10:06,800 --> 01:10:10,080
 Beneath your surface lexicon is your deep lexicon.

997
01:10:10,080 --> 01:10:16,480
 Your deep lexicon is made up of those 20,000 to 35,000 words you recognize but don't

998
01:10:16,480 --> 01:10:17,480
 use.

999
01:10:17,480 --> 01:10:24,440
 We know this because you recognize most if not all words that articulate speakers use.

1000
01:10:24,440 --> 01:10:29,600
 Why is it then that you struggle to think of those same words in conversation?

1001
01:10:29,600 --> 01:10:33,200
 Because those words don't register in your surface lexicon.

1002
01:10:33,200 --> 01:10:36,160
 This introduces the second rule of articulation.

1003
01:10:36,160 --> 01:10:40,960
 Effortless articulation is limited to the size of your surface lexicon.

1004
01:10:40,960 --> 01:10:47,200
 Now this does not mean inflating your surface lexicon with flowery, sophisticated words.

1005
01:10:47,200 --> 01:10:52,880
 Articulate speakers recognize that you can use ordinary words and still be articulate.

1006
01:10:52,880 --> 01:10:55,840
 Think of your lexicon as a box of crayons.

1007
01:10:55,840 --> 01:11:00,720
 You can easily use a wide color palette to design a vibrant picture or you can create

1008
01:11:00,720 --> 01:11:05,920
 something profound with just three colors or choose an uncommon color to substitute for

1009
01:11:05,920 --> 01:11:07,120
 a common one.

1010
01:11:07,120 --> 01:11:09,200
 The same is true with your words.

1011
01:11:09,200 --> 01:11:16,280
 You don't always have to prostitute ordinary words down to obvious and common meanings.

1012
01:11:16,280 --> 01:11:20,520
 The third problem eroding my speaking was thought retention.

1013
01:11:20,520 --> 01:11:26,120
 And you can't retain a thought in your mind for longer than 10 seconds.

1014
01:11:26,120 --> 01:11:31,280
 Your speech is like a kite following every new wind current never climbing high in the

1015
01:11:31,280 --> 01:11:32,280
 sky.

1016
01:11:32,280 --> 01:11:37,400
 Articulate speakers are able to hold a thought in their mind as they work to peel away

1017
01:11:37,400 --> 01:11:40,400
 the layers that lead to understanding.

1018
01:11:40,400 --> 01:11:43,200
 You can't grip a thought in your mind.

1019
01:11:43,200 --> 01:11:48,880
 You're thinking and speaking become superficial and scrambled.

1020
01:11:48,880 --> 01:11:51,120
 This is the third rule of Articulacy.

1021
01:11:51,120 --> 01:11:55,640
 The longer you engage a thought, the greater depth you achieve with words.

1022
01:11:55,640 --> 01:12:01,280
 These are the three thorns in our tongue that prevent us from producing Articulate speech.

1023
01:12:01,280 --> 01:12:02,520
 How did we come to this?

1024
01:12:02,520 --> 01:12:04,840
 Why do we battle with these issues?

1025
01:12:04,840 --> 01:12:09,880
 One day I remember asking myself this after a particularly frustrating time of sounding

1026
01:12:09,880 --> 01:12:11,400
 juvenile and vague.

1027
01:12:11,400 --> 01:12:16,120
 I went to YouTube and researched the most Articulate speakers I could find.

1028
01:12:16,120 --> 01:12:21,680
 It was then that I had an epiphany.

1029
01:12:21,680 --> 01:12:27,960
 Was my substandard speech the result of poor language inputs?

1030
01:12:27,960 --> 01:12:33,640
 I asked myself that question and wondered how much would I have to change the sources

1031
01:12:33,640 --> 01:12:38,280
 of language I expose myself to in order to influence my speech.

1032
01:12:38,280 --> 01:12:44,560
 To understand how my language was potentially being invaded by poor language sources, I

1033
01:12:44,560 --> 01:12:49,360
 began writing down each source of language that I expose myself to for more than 10 minutes

1034
01:12:49,360 --> 01:12:50,360
 a day.

1035
01:12:50,360 --> 01:12:55,760
 I rated each on a scale of 1 to 10 on how Articulate and Intelligence I considered each to be.

1036
01:12:55,760 --> 01:12:59,240
 If you want to do this for yourself, you can use this worksheet that's freely linked

1037
01:12:59,240 --> 01:13:00,240
 below.

1038
01:13:00,240 --> 01:13:05,920
 This exercise revealed that I didn't have any language inputs higher than a 5, which

1039
01:13:05,920 --> 01:13:09,480
 is approximately the level of Articulacy of an average conversation.

1040
01:13:09,480 --> 01:13:13,120
 I quickly realized the fourth rule of Articulacy.

1041
01:13:13,120 --> 01:13:18,020
 The quality of your speech is a product of your language environments.

1042
01:13:18,020 --> 01:13:25,240
 The books you read, videos you watch, music you listen to, etch, language patterns in your

1043
01:13:25,240 --> 01:13:29,320
 mind that unconsciously are imitated in your speech.

1044
01:13:29,320 --> 01:13:33,720
 And since most people aren't Articulate or thoughtful with their words, you're constantly

1045
01:13:33,720 --> 01:13:39,960
 exposed to high doses of vague, vapid, and oftentimes vulgar language.

1046
01:13:39,960 --> 01:13:47,320
 Your surface lexicon is the size it is because you encounter those 500 to 1500 words daily.

1047
01:13:47,320 --> 01:13:53,800
 When we first learn language as children, we absorb 3 to 5 new words every day.

1048
01:13:53,800 --> 01:13:59,200
 By adulthood, it takes a deliberate effort to expose ourselves to new and creative word

1049
01:13:59,200 --> 01:14:00,200
 combinations.

1050
01:14:00,200 --> 01:14:05,480
 In fact, the battle is preventing the shrinking of our surface lexicon.

1051
01:14:05,480 --> 01:14:10,480
 Think of your surface lexicon as an expanding and contracting circle.

1052
01:14:10,480 --> 01:14:15,360
 Interacting with fresh word combinations, such as when you read a book, temporarily expands

1053
01:14:15,360 --> 01:14:18,920
 the radius of that circle to usher in those newfound words.

1054
01:14:18,920 --> 01:14:23,400
 If those words are not put to use, they lose their place within the circle as its nature

1055
01:14:23,400 --> 01:14:28,280
 is to contract to include only your most actively used words.

1056
01:14:28,280 --> 01:14:31,960
 This is why great speakers read profusely.

1057
01:14:31,960 --> 01:14:39,240
 It is to keep that language heartbeat pulsing, expanding, and fortifying their surface lexicon.

1058
01:14:39,240 --> 01:14:44,960
 We can begin to improve our verbal health with the first modification I made to my speaking,

1059
01:14:44,960 --> 01:14:47,480
 the 3 by 5 language diet.

1060
01:14:47,480 --> 01:14:53,280
 This consists of 3 5 minute sessions for a total of 15 minutes per day focused on enriching

1061
01:14:53,280 --> 01:14:54,560
 your language.

1062
01:14:54,560 --> 01:15:01,000
 The first 5 minutes is spent reading an article or book chapter that's at least one level

1063
01:15:01,000 --> 01:15:05,320
 beyond your current average language input.

1064
01:15:05,320 --> 01:15:11,320
 This exercise will expose you to richer language and help you solve poor thought retention,

1065
01:15:11,320 --> 01:15:17,120
 which is a symptom of constantly fracturing your attention like we do on social media

1066
01:15:17,120 --> 01:15:19,560
 with bite-sized content.

1067
01:15:19,560 --> 01:15:23,040
 You don't know what material to begin with, start with this list.

1068
01:15:23,040 --> 01:15:28,360
 The second 5 minutes includes reading poetry aloud with the intent of learning rhythm

1069
01:15:28,360 --> 01:15:29,360
 and cadence.

1070
01:15:29,360 --> 01:15:37,320
 There's a misnomer that articulacy is memorizing the dictionary and vomiting flower rewards.

1071
01:15:37,320 --> 01:15:38,320
 It's not.

1072
01:15:38,320 --> 01:15:43,680
 The fifth rule of articulacy is sentences sound articulate when words flow and fit into

1073
01:15:43,680 --> 01:15:44,760
 eloquent molds.

1074
01:15:44,760 --> 01:15:51,880
 If you want to learn to effortlessly fit words together in beautiful and creative ways,

1075
01:15:51,880 --> 01:15:59,080
 your speaking apparatus, your mouth, your teeth, your tongue, your jaw need to feel what

1076
01:15:59,080 --> 01:16:01,600
 it's like to say beautiful sentences.

1077
01:16:01,600 --> 01:16:07,280
 This is precisely why dead phrases are terribly difficult to prune from your speaking.

1078
01:16:07,280 --> 01:16:12,520
 You're trying to resist engaging a pattern of muscles in your mouth that's been activated

1079
01:16:12,520 --> 01:16:13,520
 a thousand times.

1080
01:16:13,520 --> 01:16:19,440
 It's like trying to change pre-cut lines on a cardboard box.

1081
01:16:19,440 --> 01:16:24,360
 When you tear the flap, it's expected to seamlessly follow the line and we're trying to tear

1082
01:16:24,360 --> 01:16:26,040
 it in a new direction.

1083
01:16:26,040 --> 01:16:30,440
 Recognize that you can create lines for other types of speaking.

1084
01:16:30,440 --> 01:16:33,120
 That's what reading poetry aloud accomplishes.

1085
01:16:33,120 --> 01:16:37,880
 Check out this list of poems linked below and I also have a popular poems playlist available

1086
01:16:37,880 --> 01:16:39,200
 on Spotify.

1087
01:16:39,200 --> 01:16:48,200
 The final 5 minutes is spent consciously attuned to the word choice of any podcast or audiobook

1088
01:16:48,200 --> 01:16:50,340
 you consume.

1089
01:16:50,340 --> 01:16:56,760
 Ignore the contents, the substance of what's being said, and visualize in your mind the words

1090
01:16:56,760 --> 01:16:57,760
 being said.

1091
01:16:57,760 --> 01:17:03,480
 This will help you understand the difference between intelligence and eloquence.

1092
01:17:03,480 --> 01:17:06,400
 Intelligence is the knowledge communicated.

1093
01:17:06,400 --> 01:17:10,360
 eloquence is how pleasing and persuasive the word sound.

1094
01:17:10,360 --> 01:17:14,800
 Here's a list of brilliant speakers who, in my opinion, have an unrivaled command of

1095
01:17:14,800 --> 01:17:15,800
 the English language.

1096
01:17:15,800 --> 01:17:21,440
 The 3x5 language formula invites the question, "How do I use the creative words and phrases

1097
01:17:21,440 --> 01:17:24,640
 I encounter to enrich my speaking?"

1098
01:17:24,640 --> 01:17:28,760
 The second modification will reduce your dependence on dead phrases.

1099
01:17:28,760 --> 01:17:34,400
 The reason why we aim to reduce dead phrases is not because they're overused.

1100
01:17:34,400 --> 01:17:43,120
 It's because they slip us into a state of mental anesthesia where we choose words without

1101
01:17:43,120 --> 01:17:44,720
 serious thought.

1102
01:17:44,720 --> 01:17:49,900
 Jesus come up from our larynx, our speaking instrument, but our brain is not as involved

1103
01:17:49,900 --> 01:17:52,760
 as if we were selecting the words for ourselves.

1104
01:17:52,760 --> 01:18:00,160
 The only way to gain back full consciousness is to descend past the dead phrase and into

1105
01:18:00,160 --> 01:18:02,040
 the heart of our thought.

1106
01:18:02,040 --> 01:18:06,200
 We accomplish this by increasing the consciousness per sentence.

1107
01:18:06,200 --> 01:18:12,080
 Think of a sentence as a mold that is filled with different levels of consciousness.

1108
01:18:12,080 --> 01:18:17,020
 The longer you think, the more saturated the words and overall sentence becomes.

1109
01:18:17,020 --> 01:18:21,880
 The more familiar a word or phrase is, the less consciousness is required to use it.

1110
01:18:21,880 --> 01:18:25,680
 Think of all the phrases and words you say automatically.

1111
01:18:25,680 --> 01:18:32,000
 Because of how little thought is required to use them, they often bruise our sentence

1112
01:18:32,000 --> 01:18:35,200
 with dull, colorless blocks.

1113
01:18:35,200 --> 01:18:40,280
 Inarticulate sentence is one where each block is vibrant and intentional.

1114
01:18:40,280 --> 01:18:46,360
 Minors can feel this, we increase our consciousness per sentence by one being on guard against dead

1115
01:18:46,360 --> 01:18:47,360
 phrases.

1116
01:18:47,360 --> 01:18:52,680
 I've included below this video a list of the common dead phrases that frequently invade

1117
01:18:52,680 --> 01:18:54,080
 our sentences.

1118
01:18:54,080 --> 01:18:59,920
 Secondly, before you commit to responding to a question or sharing your opinion, repeat

1119
01:18:59,920 --> 01:19:02,160
 the question or topic aloud.

1120
01:19:02,160 --> 01:19:06,120
 What do I think about this topic?

1121
01:19:06,120 --> 01:19:08,920
 Why do we do this?

1122
01:19:08,920 --> 01:19:15,040
 Because that topic, or the way it was phrased, is a new or at least an unfamiliar set of words.

1123
01:19:15,040 --> 01:19:21,320
 Hearing those words spoken aloud with your own mouth begins to warm up your conscious mind.

1124
01:19:21,320 --> 01:19:30,160
 Most of us cold start our speaking engine with a few familiar phrases that we cast out

1125
01:19:30,160 --> 01:19:35,200
 into the soundscape in an attempt to find an opinion worth developing.

1126
01:19:35,200 --> 01:19:37,640
 Those phrases include, "I don't know."

1127
01:19:37,640 --> 01:19:41,760
 I guess it's just like, you know, maybe I should, right, we're not thinking about what

1128
01:19:41,760 --> 01:19:42,760
 we're saying.

1129
01:19:42,760 --> 01:19:45,280
 It's not a bad answer, it's just not articulate.

1130
01:19:45,280 --> 01:19:48,080
 Remember, the first rule of articulacy.

1131
01:19:48,080 --> 01:19:52,040
 Articulacy increases when you practice conscious selection of your words.

1132
01:19:52,040 --> 01:19:56,400
 This is why pausing before speaking has long been considered a hallmark of articulate

1133
01:19:56,400 --> 01:19:57,400
 speakers.

1134
01:19:57,400 --> 01:20:02,200
 They are affording conscious thought to the sentence they're about to produce.

1135
01:20:02,200 --> 01:20:06,040
 The most unconscious part of our sentence is, is the beginning.

1136
01:20:06,040 --> 01:20:11,120
 The greatest communicators realize that the first few sentences, really the first few

1137
01:20:11,120 --> 01:20:14,400
 words, are where we are finding our opinion.

1138
01:20:14,400 --> 01:20:18,080
 Finding our opinion is like driving with a frosted windshield.

1139
01:20:18,080 --> 01:20:21,680
 It's hard to see the road ahead until the windshield is defrosted.

1140
01:20:21,680 --> 01:20:24,320
 Only then do you drive straight with confidence.

1141
01:20:24,320 --> 01:20:31,120
 The quickest way to begin warming up our conscious mind is to repeat the topic or question aloud.

1142
01:20:31,120 --> 01:20:36,440
 The final modification I made was learning the secret of intellectual humility.

1143
01:20:36,440 --> 01:20:44,360
 This is the brilliant practice of using your speaking weakness to supercharge your responses.

1144
01:20:44,360 --> 01:20:49,400
 When we listen to articulate speakers, what we perceive as them being articulate is less

1145
01:20:49,400 --> 01:20:55,960
 often about the precise, eloquent words and more about how they begin their sentences

1146
01:20:55,960 --> 01:21:03,120
 in a way that illustrates that they can creatively navigate challenging situations.

1147
01:21:03,120 --> 01:21:06,480
 Watch this clip from the American Film Director, Orson Wells.

1148
01:21:06,480 --> 01:21:10,160
 That's a better question than I have an answer for.

1149
01:21:10,160 --> 01:21:12,680
 Honestly it is.

1150
01:21:12,680 --> 01:21:13,680
 I don't know.

1151
01:21:13,680 --> 01:21:14,920
 I would want to think about it.

1152
01:21:14,920 --> 01:21:20,560
 I think my answer would be frivolous and I'd like to think about it.

1153
01:21:20,560 --> 01:21:22,560
 It's a it's a worrisome question.

1154
01:21:22,560 --> 01:21:28,000
 Listen to this clip, how candid Orson was about not having a great answer and requiring

1155
01:21:28,000 --> 01:21:29,440
 time to think.

1156
01:21:29,440 --> 01:21:34,240
 This is what makes articulate speakers so captivating their ability to be forward with

1157
01:21:34,240 --> 01:21:36,640
 their communication needs.

1158
01:21:36,640 --> 01:21:38,920
 This is the sixth rule of articulacy.

1159
01:21:38,920 --> 01:21:43,600
 And admission of limitation is often the most articulate answer.

